  All stored rows (main_12.csv and main_128.csv) share ONE split; this machine produces another.
  Cause: main.cpp builds the fixed split with std::shuffle(perm, mt19937(SPLIT_SEED)). std::shuffle is not
  specified to yield the same permutation across standard-library implementations. The stored data came from
  _to_delete/main_linux_binary (ELF aarch64, GNU/Linux, libstdc++); this machine uses libc++. Same seed,
  different permutation, therefore a different 45k/5k/10k split and different weight init.
  CONSEQUENCE: old and new rows must not be mixed inside one file or one paired comparison.
    - main_12.csv is NOT extended to seeds 21-30; results/main_12_v2.csv gets seeds 1-30, all re-run here.
    - main_128.csv is NOT extended to seeds 7-20; results/main_128_v2.csv gets seeds 1-20, all re-run here.
    - every pre-existing CSV stays untouched as the historical record.
  No training semantics changed; this is a build/platform property of the existing code.
